s=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.854em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0572em;\">j</span></span></span></span></span></span> are substitutes at <span class=\"katex-lite\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"(p,u),\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">p</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">u</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span></span></span></span></span> is it always the case that goods <span class=\"katex-lite\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"j\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.854em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0572em;\">j</span></span></span></span></span></span> and <span class=\"katex-lite\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"i\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6595em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">i</span></span></span></span></span></span> are substitutes at <span class=\"katex-lite\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"(p,u)\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">p</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">u</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span></span></span></span>?</p></div><p class=\"pq-none\">No machine-checked answer for this part.</p></div></li><li class=\"pq-part\"><span class=\"pq-l\" aria-label=\"Part (b)\">b</span><div class=\"pq-pq\"><div class=\"ue-math\"><p>Give an example of two goods for which it is plausible to think that they would be substitutes for a consumer at one price and not substitutes at another price.</p></div><p class=\"pq-none\">No machine-checked answer for this part.</p></div></li><li class=\"pq-part\"><span class=\"pq-l\" aria-label=\"Part (c)\">c</span><div class=\"pq-pq\"><div class=\"ue-math\"><p>By considering what happens when the price of good <span class=\"katex-lite\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"i\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6595em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">i</span></span></span></span></span></span> increases (and using the symmetry of the Slutsky matrix), show that every good has at least one substitute.</p></div><p class=\"pq-none\">No machine-checked answer for this part.</p></div></li><li class=\"pq-part\"><span class=\"pq-l\" aria-label=\"Part (d)\">d</span><div class=\"pq-pq\"><div class=\"ue-math\"><p>Assume that coffee and tea are substitutes at all <span class=\"katex-lite\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"(p,u).\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">p</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">u</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span><span class=\"mord\">.</span></span></span></span></span> How does the amount a consumer will pay to avoid being deprived of tea altogether depend on whether or not coffee is available? Prove your answer.</p></div><p class=\"pq-none\">No machine-checked answer for this part.</p></div></li></ol><div class=\"pq-ans pq-ans--whole\" data-answer><p class=\"pq-ans-k\">Answer</p><div class=\"ue-math\"><p>As printed, this does not hold in full generality. Counterexample: with a single good there is no other good to be its substitute. Other counterexamples use a negative price, or a level that no bundle reaches, so that Hicksian demand is empty. The corrected statement adds at least two goods, positive prices, and a Hicksian demand that is a single bundle near <span class=\"katex-lite\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"p\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.625em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">p</span></span></span></span></span></span> and differentiable at <span class=\"katex-lite\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"p.\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.625em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">p</span></span><span class=\"mord\">.</span></span></span></span></span></p></div></div>"}